#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Num
{
private:
    int m_x;

public:
    Num(int val = 0) : m_x(val) {}

    // Friend functions
    friend istream& operator>>(istream& in, Num& obj);
    friend ostream& operator<<(ostream& out, const Num& obj);
};

// Overload >>
istream& operator>>(istream& in, Num& obj)
{
    cout << "Enter value: ";
    in >> obj.m_x;
    return in;
}

// Overload <<
ostream& operator<<(ostream& out, const Num& obj)
{
    out << obj.m_x;
    return out;
}

int main()
{
    Num ob;

    cin >> ob;

    cout << "Value = " << ob << endl;

    return 0;
}


=========================================================
STREAM INSERTION (<<) & EXTRACTION (>>) INTERVIEW Qs ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

Q1. Why are operator<< and operator>> usually friend functions?

Answer:
Because the left operand is ostream/istream, not our class.
Friend allows access to private members.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Can operator<< be a member function?

Answer:
No.

cout << obj

is interpreted as

operator<<(cout, obj);

Since cout belongs to ostream, we cannot add member functions
to the ostream class.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Why does operator<< return ostream&?

Answer:
To support chaining.

Example:

cout << obj1 << obj2 << endl;

---------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Why does operator>> return istream&?

Answer:
To support chaining.

Example:

cin >> obj1 >> obj2;

---------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Why pass const Class& to operator<<?

Answer:

✓ Avoid object copy
✓ Prevent modification
✓ Better performance

---------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Why pass Class& to operator>>?

Answer:

Because operator>> modifies the object's data.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Why not return ostream instead of ostream&?*****

Answer:

Returning by value would copy the stream, which is not allowed.
Streams are non-copyable.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Why shouldn't we print prompts inside operator>>?

Answer:

Overloaded operators should only perform input/output.
User prompts should be written by the caller.

Bad:
operator>>()
{
    cout << "Enter value";
}

Good:
cout << "Enter value: ";
cin >> obj;

---------------------------------------------------------

Q9. What is the actual function call for cout << obj?

Answer:

operator<<(cout, obj);

---------------------------------------------------------

Q10. What is the actual function call for cin >> obj?

Answer:

operator>>(cin, obj);

=========================================================
INTERVIEW ONE-LINERS
=========================================================

✓ operator<< is generally a friend function.
✓ Return ostream&/istream& to enable chaining.
✓ Pass object by const reference for output.
✓ Pass object by non-const reference for input.
✓ Keep overloaded operators free of user prompts.



